From 74c80dcfbf2de005bfd157ff1c4d5e50a1ccfc60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:48:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nvk/rm, wsi: presents retire on RM fences, no CPU wait (S4)

Until now every present on the Helios scanout waited on the CPU for the
frame's GPU work (the WSI's wait_before_present, the Helios UMD's frame
gate) and then flipped.  Now the present hands the flip an RM fence and
returns at once (guest/windows/docs/rm-fence-marker.md, dxvk-on-nvk S4):

- A per-queue "present timeline": a 64-bit semaphore, one entry of an RM
  NV_SEMAPHORE_SURFACE over 4 KiB of RM-allocated system memory (RM must
  map it itself; an OS descriptor is refused with NV_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED),
  imported on the host render node as a nvidia-drm semaphore-surface fence
  context (SEMSURF_FENCE_CTX_CREATE, one per entry).  It is an ordinary
  nvkmd_rm_sync whose value lives in the surface; a context binds its
  channel to the surface (NV_SEMAPHORE_SURFACE_CTRL_CMD_BIND_CHANNEL, host
  non-stall notifier) before its first release into it, so RM checks the
  surface's waiters on the NON_STALL_INTERRUPT the release already raises.
- Per present: vk_queue_signal_sync() (now exported by the runtime) of the
  timeline's next value after everything submitted to the queue so far,
  then SEMSURF_FENCE_CREATE for that value: a backend handle that fires one
  host EventReady when the GPU writes it (KMD 22.22.311 records it as ours).
- The fenced flip (nvkmd_rm_mem_scanout_flip_fenced): with KMD capability
  bit 32 (HELIOS_NVRM_CAP_SCANOUT_FENCE) SCANOUT_PRESENT carries the fence
  (flag RM_FENCE, handle at offset 52) and the KMD sends the flip when it
  fires; QUEUE_FULL waits for our own fence and retries, so a plain flip
  never overtakes queued ones.  Without the capability (KMD 22.22.311) a
  flip thread of the device waits on the fence (EVENT_REGISTER, no
  polling) and sends a plain SCANOUT_PRESENT, in order, at most eight
  frames behind.  Without DRM fences or a new enough librmclient: the CPU
  wait as before.
- WSI: wsi_device::win32.scanout_flip_fenced (optional).  A scanout
  swapchain with it skips the CPU wait before queue_present
  (wsi_swapchain::gpu_ordered_present) and hands the present queue to the
  driver; VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT falls back to the wait.
- helios_icd_interface version 3 (appended, same export): queue_rm_fence,
  rm_fence_wait, rm_fence_close, scanout_present_fenced, caps RM_FENCE,
  SCANOUT_FENCE_KMD and PRESENT_FENCE_KMD, for the Helios D3D11 UMD
  (feat/s4-rm-fences).
- librmclient: crm_win_caps, crm_win_semsurf_ctx_create,
  crm_win_semsurf_fence_create, crm_win_fence_wait,
  crm_win_scanout_present_fenced, all optional.

NVK_RM_FENCE=0 turns it off; NVK_RM_FENCE_KMD=0 keeps fences from the KMD
(flip thread).

win11, KMD 22.22.311 (no capability bit 32, so the flip thread), RTX 5090,
vk_rmfence_test: no fence ever fired before the submit's own fence
(checked every round); 2 GiB fill: wake 2091 us after submit with the RM
fence vs 2157 us with vkWaitForFences; scanout 1920x1080, three images,
1 GiB fill per frame: 808 -> 982 fps, the presenting thread's time in
present 1228 -> 73 us per frame; 64 MiB fill: 3711 -> 6481 fps.
vk_scanout_present (trivial triangle) through the WSI: 5645 fps with the
CPU wait, 4899 fps fenced (a fence costs one escape, about 60-80 us, which a
trivial frame does not repay).  KMD counters after 38561 fences: NvFence =
NvFenceCl = NvFenceSig, NvFenceErr 0.

Order: after 0029 (applies with 0022, 0023, 0025 and 0027-0029 before it),
before patches-windows-dxvk/.
